  "partyLabels": "none-recorded",
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919: the first county council election in the five electoral areas that replaced the county electoral divisions of 1899, 21 seats. Polled Thursday 3 June 1920 (Belfast News-Letter, 3 June 1920, p. 6, \"To-day's Polling\"); counted at the Courthouse, Crumlin Road, Belfast, by Alexander Millar, secretary of the county council, as returning officer. Carrickfergus area (four seats): 4,751 valid votes, quota 951; 14,027 electors. Smith, Miscampbell and Wilson (exactly the quota) were elected on the first count. Count 2, Smith's surplus of 379: M'Meekin 224, Coey 134, Morrow 21. Count 3, Miscampbell's surplus of 89: Coey 56, M'Meekin 25, Morrow 8. Count 4, Morrow's 403 votes: M'Meekin to 984 and Coey to 613 (86 and 16; the paper prints the totals only, and 301 is the remainder). M'Meekin was elected on the fourth count. Edward Coey D.L., a retiring member, was defeated. The minute of the council's first meeting also records the chairmen of the rural district councils, who sat ex officio, and the co-opted members; they were not elected and are not listed here."
